\page LINC mode assembly (as used on LINC, LINC-8 and PDP-12 computers) is enabled by the use of certain command-line options; the implementation is generally compatible with the \ul\i LAP6-DIAL\ulnone\i0 /\ul\i DIAL-MS\ulnone\i0  embedded assembler utility.\cf2  \cf1 I have a problem with this as well:  The loadable BINARY is needed on the matching systems ultimately to be meaningful, but the assembly can be done on any\par
P?S/8 system and then the binary at least is transferred to the appropriate P?S/8 system as possible and required if feasible.  Transferring binary data to an original LINC is a very roundabout process not worth going into, but the point is it *is* a very roudablut process; at least in the other two cases, there is a good possibility a P?S/8 system can deliver the binary to the hardware that needs it, etc.  The systems that run on the hardware might not be capable of much more than loading the binary, which it is presumed they *are* capable of by definition.  But a very long full-blown 4K LINC-8 program complete with PROGOFOP is not likely capable of being assembed on the target hardware without more memory to support the assembly itself; this is often the case with certain very detailed programs in general that use many symbols as a matter of good practice, etc.  It is often the case that a truly complex 4K program needs much more memory than the binary runs on to assemble the source code.  When you add in all the overhead of supporting dual assembly, the bar is raised, etc.  Back in the day, I needed all of the 8K machine I was running the 4K binary application on to assemble the source code that loaded into 4K. In some cases, some of the next 4K was also used as a data area for the customer's suite of applications, etc.  But this is a subtle point of the nature of the beast.  Enabling options of the assembler means less symbols in 4K and more needing more memory to assemble the code, etc.  Classic LINC is not at all compatible with the PDP-8, just that the LINC-8 and PDP-12 are capable of running unstated utilities to transfer the data to the "standard" LINCtape format which neither OS/8 nor P?S/8 can use in the ordinary sense, but the tape hardware on the LINC can ONLY read the standard format, so some unstated means is needed to indirectly get the binary to the classic linc if that is the mission; P?S/8 is where P?S/8 PAL is that is used to develop the source code into the binary in slurp format, and then a slurp loader places it in memory, presumably as part of some unstated block-writing utility cognizant of what to do.  \par
\par
