Clearly, P?S/8 cannot even be there, so the point is that when there is a LINC-type requirement, the most accurate description points out the potential need to cause the binary to be used "beyond" P?S/8, and in the case of the LINC, it is NOT even paper-tape compatible in any sense.  P?S/8 running on either of the other two is capable of working with the binary of the unstated writing blocks utility in conjunction with the assembly of the program using P?S/8 PAL to indirectly get it to the proper target architecture.  In fact, the assembly requires the user to modify a few key symbols because their definition is model-dependent among the three, etc.  So,somehow the need for a Linctape media-based export utility might be needded.  But the absolute truth is the assembly portion can be done on any\par
P?S/8 system that has the memory capability as dictated by the exact usage and the ability to handle the appropriate files completely, which is not 100% a given every time.  Also, all LINC machines are 2K not 4K as an aside.  In theory LINC-8 programs should go up to 31K if PROOFOP is involved, a virtual certainty of the program is classlc LINC with emulation, while on the PDP-12, the full 32 K does happen; due to unexpected hardware problems, as a practical matter, no LINC-8 systems exceed 8K and they have to be slowed down and retuned and 8K-specific updates applied that are NOT to be done to a 4K LINC-8.  For example, the 4K LINC-8 uses the identical PDP-8 crystal clock, it has to be changed to a pin-compatible adjustable clock and adjusted critically, etc.  Thus, to not mixup the relevant application and that which accomplishes what, please dream up some appropriate shorter verbiage to explain the potential mixup and correct it to be bulletproof, etc.  Too much explanation; this really is a small point, but it is a valid one.\line\line The need for symbol redefinition is that there are a small few instructions that were defined for the PDP-12 on P?S/8 PAL, but the conventions on a few are different both on the LINC and on the LINC-8 each to the other. Some the LINC and LINC-8 are the same but different from the PDP-12.  I point out somewhere below that only a reassembly of P?S/8 PAL could accommodate this naturally, but this is a small consideration as a sophisticated program aware of the three variant models should define the relevant symbols for the variant being addressed.  [Ironically, most of the cases actually work anyway, but they are not DOCUMENTED to do so.  This is getting too much into the weeds, but there are hardware projects that co-opt some of these obscure symbols to implement such as parts of the TC12F option to help the PDP-12 read and write DECtapes AND the color change instructions if you have the advanced VR20 bi-color superset of the VR12/VR14. The only instructions to commandere are these "extra" operate instructions thus, there is always the PROPER instruction definition to use, but on some machines you can get away with it.  Proper programming technique works with KNOWLEDGE and not by happenstance that might not work on another machine, etc.\par
