Note: For Maury, there is an entirely different level to the problem:  The LINC software for the most part and maybe 30 percent of the oldest LINC type software does NOT use ASCII text, but instead the classic LINC text set as generated by its weird keyboard.  PROGOFOP can translate teleype keys for these older systems and there are a lot of little legend strip printouts to help, etc.  There was even keycap kits and a typewheel change so the 33 Teletype looks a bit more like the LINC and assumes PROGOFOP is in effect.\line\line Fortunatly the newer LAP systems eventually went ASCII and thus more like DIAL, such as LAP6W aka WISAL.  It's pretty piss-poor, but modestly better than what came before, etc.\line\line Thus, another level of Maury's project is there is no other assembler for this source code and of course no nice way to get a good listing of it without some translation TECO macro or whatever, and then due to the limitations of these early systems, commentary is meager at best.\line\line Thus, the proper treatment for his historical tapes is to list them somehow "as is" but also to translate the, into DIAL and P?S/8 PAL compatible ASCII chaacter-based replacement assembly source code.  Once that is done, we could discuss a post-processing utility to LINC-codize the printout to resembler what the LINC software NEVER could but it would be nice if magic was performed.  I think there could be as many as a dozen symbols that are different!\par
