The program input text to PAL is generally the concatenated contents of a group of \ul\i Tiny File System\ulnone\i0  (\i TFS\i0 ) files passed in the proper order within the input portion of the command.  Each TFS file is exactly 2048 12-bit words long; in general, each file will tend to contain up to 3/4 of this capacity as six-bit bytes in the P?S/8 ASCII text subset character set.  This corresponds to about 3000 characters per TFS file depending on average line width and attendant line number requirements.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 TFS files include line numbers which are ignored by P?S/8 PAL.  Outside of the edit buffer of the keyboard monitor (which can modify the text contents and line numbers) these line numbers are permanent.  Most P?S/8 system programs ignore line number content within input files with the exception of several utilities specifically designed to display or otherwise interact with line numbers (present only in TFS text files, but not in extended-length text files).\cf3   This may be a dumb question, but if input files are typically concatenated TFS files with line numbers, how are files from non-P?S/8 PDP-8 systems (i.e., those with no TFS line numbers) input & assembled?  Does/do the input file(s) _have_ to be TFS formatted files? \cf1 You are confused here:\line\line The effective input file STREAM of CHARACTERS is by definition the content of as many input files as are used and all characters concatneated together.  This as a concept is common.  OS/8 users can specify more than one input file as well, but I think no more than something like 3 total, which is fine when the bulk of the input stream is likely in one or two files.  FOCAL is an except where it is essentially two files in a 60/40 relationhip.  Assembling Kermit-12 expects a short header file to be read in front of the main source, in fact it is so short it need not be present after pass 1 of the assembly.  Some type it in during the assembly because OS/8 has a cutesy minor obscure feature:  You can type on the command line TTY: input and then the rest of the files by name.  During pass one you type a few lines which is all that is needed here, then Control-Z to end that, and then the rest of the input stream during pass 1 is the contents of the files, etc.  When pass 2 [or 3, this is OS/8!] starts, you can opt to JUST hit Control-Z since you have to enter either nothing or a PERFECT redundancy, etc. to not further change the parameters which would make for a misassembly. This is by my clever program design more than anything.  Just because OS/8 has a cute obscurity few people actually use doesn't compel me to imitate it 100%.  As such, the former method with a short file in front effective in all passes will be used in this case of Kermit-12 and similarly structured projecsts.  That aspect of it is actually a common technique.  Also, the cutesy means you can not have a dummy page on the listing, but with a little conditional assembly tricks, I can make it not change the listing, but I do admit I never thought about it completely when I wrote that.  It can be done and when I get to make another pass on that program, I will implemenmt the method I describe here.  Also, I tend to to the few line edit in the file, which I recommend in all cases:\line\line IFNDEF FOO <FOO=40> /THIS MEANS THAT BY DEFAULT FOO BECOMES 40>\line\line but I put in front of it:\line\line FOO=42\line\line That way the conditional NEVER does anything because the explicit line defines it expliccitly.  But that's me, etc.\line\line That TFS files have line numbers is irrelevant as they are ignored by the clever overall dessign of Richard Lary.  When you examine the beginning of the TFS file, you never see any numbers.  They are at the back end going backwards.  So, if you only read the files from the front, no line numbers.  Only special programs like PRINT bother to look for line numbers and are designed to both handle them if present, and work without them.  The latter only happens with extended-length files.  OS8CON /E is used to create the files from other sources if they are OS/8-based, or L6DCON if they are LAP 6-DIAL based.  For anything else, another conversion program has to be written, so in the future, expect a P?S/8 variant to PIP10 for PDP-10 DECtapes.\line\line P?S/8 gave up support for ZONKED files, which have the existing line numbers removed so a bit more can be added, but only the new lines get new line numbers and the rest can no longer be editied.  Too kludgey, for example, does all of the new input get line numbers and is intended to be all BEFORE the zonked section or as I suspect it must, be AFTER the zonked section.  It was a bad idea, etc.  But a recently Zonked file had no line numbers, but I say it is not really a TFS text file any more, but the directory could contain the funny variants.\line\line Oddly enough, the concept of zonked files can be remedied by OS8CON but that didn't exist in R-L, perhaps part of why they were never popular; it's a kludge not really welcome.  An old grwoing pain that grew up and moved away :-)\par
