\ul 1.12 PAL output files\ulnone\par
\par
As stated above, all P?S/8 TFS files are fixed size (2048 12-bit words).  TFS binary files are created in the versatile \i Slurp\i0  binary format, which is significantly more efficient than binary files based on paper-tape frame images (such as the standard DEC \i BIN\i0  loader format).  A typical TFS binary file contains nearly 6/7 as much binary data as a block-format core-image file of identical size without implementing any form of internal block structure.  Binary loaders for TFS binary files are available on all standard P?S/8 configurations; a generic virtual \i Slurp\i0  loader is implemented in all systems.  Many systems also support a device-specific \i Slurp\i0  loader (which will be the default loader if implemented).\cf3   I think there will be a lot of questions about "all systems" and "many systems."\cf0  \cf1 Not every hardware supports the underpinnings for a hardware-dependent slurp loader.  All systems alwasy support the virtual slurp loader which is device-indepentent; it uses the system device to create a virtualized space to cover all potential loading of the protected areas of the input buffer for the slurp data, the system device handler itself, and a 4 page virtualization buffer when at any instant has in it a snapshot of one of the three possible states of the buffer.  When loading of the moment changes to addressing the needs of another buffered area, the buffer is written out to the according 4 blocks, and then the new 4 blocks are read in and are overlayed in memory with the new loadiing date for that 4 page region etc.  At the end of the assembly, the last buffer is written back where it belongs and all of memory EXCEPT the image of 07600 is read in at the end of loading, then the starting field and address are applied.  They sit inside of 07600-07777 and applied at the end: When the last of the pages is loaded [other than 07600-07777 which is saved but never loaded by the virtualizing process] the contents of 07632-07637 are set to the standard values the GET command needs so that a virtual GET command also works [remember, on most systems it is an option to go either way!].  GET is equivalent to setting the starting address to 7632 as in =7632 and the starting field to 0 [by NOT setting /1 through /7] so it will save all of memory into blocks 0020-0057 to establish the virtual core image of all of field 0 for the benefit of the ODT command, etc.  If the non-virtual slurp loader is in effect, the /I reload code reloads most of the system handler except for the starting field and address words and the JMP I that starts it up.  Either way gets you slurp loading depending on the method chosen IF it is an option.\line\line We have discussed this years ago, as an artifact of virtual loading, if there is a rogue attempt to load into 07600-07777, it will be in the virtual buffer where it is saved but never loaded.  By doing this twice, once with /Z to pre-zero everything, and again with /H to set all to the HLT instruction 7402, you can see what, if anything differs.  That identifies the rogue.\line\line Something like 4 official PDP-8/E diagnostics STUPIDLY contain rogues and cannot be loaded by OS/8 no way no how.  But P?S/8 can assuming there is some free space in memory to put the rogues in which might destroy the system handler, but it cannot otherwise be done at all.  Stupid manager allowed his programers to break long-established rules.  These programs have PDP-8 versions that don't have this crap!\line\line So, it is possible to ruin in memory P?S/8, but have the program loaded as written.  A BATCH can be contrived where you mave to manually boot past the problem points, but the suite of tests will be complete while OS/8 MUST avoid them, etc.  A kludge to be sure, but the alternative is no can do.\line\line In any case, write something to reference configurations as to whether or not a hard-dependent non-virtual slurp loader is available.  It ought to be included wherever that winds up in some appendix.\line\line tHE GENERAL rule is this:\line\line 1) Single-cycle DMA cannot.\line\line 2) Program transfer ought to have it if there is enough room.\line\line 3) Complicated program transfer needs extended memory.  If 2) is complicated, that should cover it.  The RX50 handler may be the one that cannot do it!\line\line 4) All three-cycle devices DO have a slurp loader with the exception of the RF08 because it is THAT FAST.  But a virtual loader on an RF08 is very fast anyway and the least caring configuration regarding this, etc.\line\line 5) Depending on the CPU, the DF32 slurp loader might not work and has to be tested on the actual hardware.  That said, there is an opion on the original DF32 but NOT the DF32D to interleave by 4:1 originally for the benefit of the PDP-8/S, but if that hardware option is set, slurp will work there.\line\line The code is obscure and lost, but is trivial to recreate if anyone wants it.  I can use sIMH to recreate it, but I cannot truest the timing, so it might not work OR it might be too ideal depending and won't indicate anything useful in terms of transferring the system to hardware.\line\line DF32 can at best create an inadequately small system far smaller than the capacity of one DECtape.  It's largely a toy and inadequte in OS/8 as well.\par
\par
Jim Roth made R-L work on DF32 on an 8/I, but no extended memory support which makes it slightly slower, thus unknown.  The R-L slurp loader code for TC01 is the "grandfather" of the R-L one and Jim did part of the work on that one as well, but I made the last mods to it, etc.  That one works fine. Also on sIMH!\par
