\ul 1.13 Command-line option switches\ulnone\par
\par
The command-line option switches are passed in a manner consistent with \ul\i Concise Command Language\ulnone\i0  (\i CCL\i0 ) as implemented in TOPS-10 and OS/8.  Two variant forms are supported as follows:\par
\par
\tab 1) One or more individual option switches each preceded by \cf4\highlight5 /\cf0\highlight0  such\par
\tab    as:\par
\par
\tab    /M /N /L\par
\par
\tab 2) Grouped switches preceded by \cf4\highlight5 (\cf0\highlight0  such as:\par
\par
\tab    (MNL)\par
\par
\tab    Note: The trailing \cf4\highlight5 )\cf0\highlight0  is optional unless needed to delimit and\par
\tab    separate the option group from another command-line section.\par
\par
Both forms of option switches can be used together in the same command; all option switches may be given redundantly without error.\par
\par
\ul 1.14 Passed numerical value\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL can optionally chain to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0 , the binary file loading and utility program, for the purpose of loading (or paper-tape punching) the binary files created during the assembly.  If desired, the assembled program can be automatically started at an address expressed as =\i xxxx\i0  where \i xxxx\i0  is up to four octal digits in the range of 0000 through 7776; for programs starting in extended memory, the /1 through /7 switches provide the starting field.  See Sections 2.02 and 2.30 below for more information.\par
\par
The P?S/8 keyboard monitor internally passes the value 7777 when an explicit numerical value is not provided.  When binary file loading is in effect, BIN uses this value to cause program start at a system-specific location somewhere within the system kernel area (07600-07777); a safe halt instruction is loaded at the selected location.  Pressing \cf4\highlight5 Continue\cf0\highlight0  on the front panel will effectively loop in place preventing potential problems; the user is expected to start the program manually.  \cf3 What do you do when you don't have a front panel?  How do DECmate users "press" the equivalent of the \highlight6 Continue\highlight0  switch?\par
