The following sections of this document describe command-line options grouped by common function.  External references to sorted command-line option lists and additional information are provided elsewhere in this document.  Appendix D below contains a list of all command line options in sorted order.\par
\par
\ul 2.01 Output file options\ulnone\par
\par
If explicit binary output files are not given in the command line, the following option switches can be used:\par
\par
\tab /B The \ul\i %\ulnone\i0  file on the P?S/8 system device bootup logical unit\par
\tab    (see description of the /U option switch below) is used as\par
 \tab    the (first) binary output file.\par
 \par
\tab /D If the /B option switch is set, the \ul\i $\ulnone\i0  file on the P?S/8\par
\tab    system device bootup logical unit (see description of the /U\par
 \tab    option switch below) is used as the second binary output file\par
\tab    (if required).\par
\par
\tab /U Change the logical unit that applies (when the /B and /D\par
\tab    option switches are used as described above) from the system\par
 \tab    device bootup logical unit to the result of the following\par
 \tab    calculation: \{\{System device bootup logical unit\} \cf4\highlight5 XOR\cf0\highlight0  1\}.\par
\tab    This selects the \i opposite\i0  logical unit of the primary pair of\par
 \tab    logical drive units.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Certain P?S/8 systems can be booted to non-zero logical\par
\tab    units; as such, this implementation method will generally\par
\tab    allow a useful drive pairing.\par
\par
Due to their physical proximity to the beginning of the storage device, specifying the \ul %\ulnone  and \ul $\ulnone  files can greatly speed up various\par
system operations.  Additionally, these files are not subject to the overhead of directory operations with regard to the TFS directory on\par
the same logical device unit; as such, many \tab\cf3 <--what is supposed to be here?  anything?  (a space & a tab are there)\par
\cf1 No, nothing at all!  Read the original again!\cf3\par
\par
\par
 \cf0 operations have significantly lower overhead when these files are used strategically.  On tape-based systems, throughput can be further improved by using a logical unit different from the system bootup logical unit.\par
\par
Note: The /B, /D and /U option switches are provided to simplify the\par
process of allocating output files, as most assemblies require one or two output files.  All file specifications can designate an optional device unit; as such, any combination of files can explicitly state associated logical device units as necessary.  The use of these option switches merely allows specifying commonly used output file combinations in a more succinct form.\par
\par
If more than two binary output files are required, or the created binary files should be written to permanently named TFS files, the\par
command line must include explicitly stated output files.\page Note: If the second binary output file is not required, the /D option switch will be ignored (effectively reducing the command-line to have\par
been given with only the /B option switch); the report given at the end of the assembly indicates the total count of binary output files actually processed.\par
