\par
\f0\tab A second assembly pass is performed if binary output files are \tab created and/or assembly listing output is produced.  During pass \tab two, any additional error messages will be displayed on the \tab prevailing output device (system console or lineprinter) in a \tab format similar to that of PAL10 and PAL8 (including the nearest\par
\tab symbol plus any numerical offset) to facilitate source code\par
\tab development; in certain instances additional information may be\par
\tab displayed.\par
\par
\tab Programmers may embellish the displayed output of the assembly \tab process when critical sections are being processed as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) P?S/8 PAL supports the ERROR directive; this causes a\par
 \tab    deliberate assembly error.  By using the ERROR directive, the\par
\tab    programmer can design internal safeguards to prevent logical\par
\tab    inconsistencies that might occur during program maintenance.\par
\par
\tab    The design of safeguarded code sections generally includes the\par
\tab    use of conditional assembly statements to calculate\par
\tab    problematic scenarios; as long as certain specific\par
\tab    requirements are met, there will be no output associated with \tab    the ERROR directive.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The output of the ERROR directive is consistent with\par
\tab    that of all standard errors during each assembly pass and can\par
\tab    include the display of an optional evaluated argument.\par
\tab    Automatic binary loading by chaining to P?S/8 BIN will be \tab\tab    aborted if the ERROR directive was processed.\par
\par
\tab b) P?S/8 PAL supports the PAUSE directive both as originally\par
\tab    implemented in PAL III (to ignore the rest of the contents of\par
\tab    the latest input file) and also as a language extension\par
\tab    feature which can embellish displayed output during the\par
\tab    assembly process.\page\tab    When the PAUSE directive is used with an argument, the\par
 \tab    argument is evaluated as a 12-bit unsigned value.  Displayed\par
 \tab    output is consistent with the format of standard error\par
 \tab    messages; however, PAUSE usage does not report as an error.\par
\par
\tab    The programmer can utilize the PAUSE directive for a variety\par
\tab    of purposes (such as announcing the assembly pass or start of\par
 \tab    assembly of a critical section).  When a logical inconsistency\par
\tab    is detected, the PAUSE directive can be used to output\par
\tab    additional information about the error to help maintenance\par
\tab    programmers understand the nature of the problem.\par
\par
\tab    The techniques described above and other features unique to\par
 \tab    P?S/8 PAL can be qualified to prevent use with incompatible\par
 \tab    assemblers.\par
