 \tab    permanent symbols and assembly directives.\page\tab    Note: If dual-mode (PDP-8 and LINC) assembly is in effect, the\par
\tab    symbol table will be set to that which applies at the end of\par
\tab    the assembly; certain symbols with dual definitions will\par
\tab    display values as used during the final assembly mode.\par
\par
\tab    Certain permanent symbols may have been redefined during the\par
\tab    assembly by source code statements; this is especially\par
\tab    important in LINC mode assembly where there may be multiple\par
\tab    definitions of certain instructions that are model-dependent\par
\tab    within the various LINC hardware implementations.\par
\par
\tab    While all default \i LMODE\i0  definitions within P?S/8 PAL are for\par
 \tab    the PDP-12 LINC variant, source code changes can be made to\par
\tab    redefine the affected symbols for compatibility with the\par
\tab    LINC-8 or classic LINC as required.  Permanent change of\par
\tab    certain \i SKP\i0  class or other LINC instructions can only be made\par
\tab    at the source-code level of P?S/8 PAL; as such, it is\par
\tab    recommended that all affected symbols be redefined in user\par
 \tab    source code statements as required.\par
\par
\ul 2.05 Listing options\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /L Assembly listing output will be produced.\par
\par
\tab    Unlike PAL10 and PAL8, P?S/8 PAL performs at most two passes.\par
\tab    P?S/8 PAL is capable of simultaneously creating binary file\par
\tab    and listing output during pass two; as such, the following\par
\tab    situation can be avoided:\par
\par
\tab    Obscure PAL language quirks can be problematic when literals\par
\tab    are in effect.  Novice programmers must avoid certain\par
\tab    scenarios where the definition of certain symbols and values\par
\tab    is dependent on the number of passes performed as well as\par
\tab    specific addresses of generated literals.\par
\par
\tab    While this is very bad programming technique (and avoided by\par
\tab    experienced programmers), it can be confusing to debug\par
\tab    mystifying results obtained from PAL8; PAL8 binary output is\par
\tab    created using symbolic definitions obtained at the end of pass\par
\tab    one possibly updated during pass two.  However, PAL8 listing\par
\tab    output is created using symbolic definitions obtained at the\par
\tab    end of pass two and possibly updated during pass three.\par
\par
\tab    Unless the programmer is careful to avoid pass-dependent\par
\tab    definitions, the binary output may differ from the values\par
\tab    indicated in the listing output (which may appear correct).\par
\tab    Since P?S/8 PAL always creates all output during pass two,\par
\tab    there cannot be pass-dependent differences between binary\par
\tab    output and listing output.  Relevant programming blunders will\par
\tab    be discovered sooner using P?S/8 PAL (along with additional\par
\tab    experience when the improper techniques are recognized and\par
\tab    avoided).\page\tab /P Enable wide-printing listing output.  Note: This option also\par
\tab    affects symbol table output allowing additional columns of\par
\tab    printed symbols per page.  (The expected output format changes\par
\tab    from 72/80 columns to 132 columns as implemented on\par
\tab    lineprinters that use 11" x 17" ledger/tabloid paper stock.)\par
\par
\tab    Note: PAL8 only outputs to a minimal line width to guarantee\par
\tab    compatibility with narrow-carriage devices such as the LP01\par
\tab    lineprinter, despite the fact that most printers support 132\par
\tab    column mode; outputting to an LP01 may cause excessive\par
\tab    truncation.\par
\par
\tab    P?S/8 PAL supports source statements significantly wider than\par
\tab    PAL8.  Where relevant, the listing output of P?S/8 PAL will\par
\tab    avoid truncation unlike PAL8 when assembling the identical\par
\tab    source code.\par
\par
\tab    Note: PAL8 users must exercise caution when creating\par
\tab    (somewhat) wide program statements.  Due to quirks in the PAL8\par
\tab    implementation, the precise number of characters leading to\par
\tab    truncation problems varies with statement type and other\par
\tab    unexpected issues; depending on specific circumstances, a\par
\tab    portion of the intended source statement may be truncated.\par
\tab    This can unexpectedly change logical program flow.\par
\par
\tab    Warning: This is not merely a matter of truncating the\par
\tab    listing output; rather, part of the source code statement may\par
\tab    be ignored.  Debugging unexpectedly truncated assembly\par
\tab    statements may be difficult (especially when conditional\par
\tab    assembly techniques are used in conjunction with the XLIST\par
\tab    directive).\par
\par
\tab /T Prevent output to the system lineprinter.  All output is\par
\tab    directed to the system console terminal.  See the description\par
\tab    of the /N option switch below.\par
\par
\tab /N Output \i niceties\i0  are applied to the listing output including\par
\tab    titles on each printed page.  If output is directed to the\par
\tab    system console (instead of the system lineprinter), the output\par
\tab    will produce simulated form-feeds by the use of tear-off lines\par
\tab    instead of \highlight7 <FF>\highlight0  characters; this will ensure compatibility\par
\tab    with system console devices that use roll paper such as the\par
\tab    Teletype Model 33.\par
\par
\tab    Note: For systems lacking a lineprinter that support hard copy\par
\tab    console terminals such as the LA36 (which can properly support\par
\tab    \highlight7 <FF>\highlight0  characters), the \ul\i Logical Console Overlay\ulnone\i0  can be\par
\tab    configured to redirect lineprinter output to the same physical\par
\tab    device as that used for console output.\par
\par
\tab    When the /N option switch is set, one additional page of\par
\tab    memory is allocated.  See Section 2.13 below regarding\par
 \tab    memory management options for more information.\page\tab /K Enable assembly title text from each input file.\par
\par
\tab    When the /L option switch is used in conjunction with the /N\par
 \tab    option switch, the title field text for the entire assembly is\par
\tab    taken from the first line of the first file.\par
\par
\tab    Typically, assemblies are processed by concatenating the\par
\tab    contents of multiple TFS text files.  In most cases there is\par
\tab    no particular connection between program sections in different\par
\tab    files and intended assembly title changes; file boundaries are\par
\tab    merely a file contents consideration.\par
\par
\tab    To maintain compatibility with PAL10 and PAL8, taking the\par
\tab    title field characters from the first line of every input file\par
\tab    can be enabled; the /K option switch must be given along with\par
\tab    the /L and /N option switches.  As long as each logically\par
\tab    independent section of a program is contained within either an\par
\tab    extended-length file (or a single TFS file such as a small\par
\tab    parameter setup file), meaningful title changes can be\par
\tab    obtained.\par
\par
\tab    As a practical matter, intentional use of assembly title field\par
\tab    updates via file breaks is used infrequently.  For example,\par
\tab    the source code of \ul\i FOCAL, 1969\ulnone\i0  updates the title field only\par
\tab    once during the entire assembly; as such, two extended-length\par
\tab    files (for the two main modules known as \i FOCAL\i0  and \i FLOAT\i0  \par
\tab    respectively) support the intended title field changes during\par
\tab    the assembly.\par
\tab\tab\par
\tab    Additionally, the title on the assembly can also be changed\par
\tab    using other methods that are independent of file boundaries.\par
\tab    See the description of the \i EJECT\i0  and \i TITLE\i0  directives in\par
\tab    Appendix B of this document for further information.\par
\par
\tab    Note: A two-pass assembly will occur because the /L option\par
\tab    switch is set to create listing output.  As such, the /K\par
\tab    option switch should only be used to enable title field text\par
\tab    from every input file.  The alternate usage of the /K switch\par
\tab    (to force a two-pass assembly) is moot.\par
\par
\tab /X Enable the creation of cross-reference output by loading the \par
\tab    cross-reference module; this will output all symbolic and\par
 \tab    literal references (if any) as well as the statement number\par
 \tab    associated with the reference.  Cross-reference data is\par
\tab    created in statement order during the assembly; at the end of\par
\tab    the assembly the data is sorted and included as part of the\par
\tab    overall listing output.\par
\par
\tab    Enabling cross-reference output causes all listing output to\par
 \tab    be somewhat wider because all printed lines start with the\par
 \tab    lowest statement number on the line.  (The PAL language allows\par
\tab    multiple statements on a line separated by \highlight7 ;\highlight0 .)  Statement\par
\tab    numbers must be in the range of 1 through 99999.\page\tab    Note: It is strongly recommended that listing output with\par
 \tab    statement numbering be printed on a 132 column lineprinter,\par
 \tab    especially if the /P option is also enabled.\par
\par
\tab    If the /A option switch is also enabled, all symbolic \tab\tab\tab    references will generate cross-reference output; the default\par
 \tab    action is to reference only user symbols.\par
\par
\tab    When the /X option switch is enabled, two additional pages of\par
\tab    memory are allocated.  See Section 2.13 of this document\par
\tab    regarding memory management options for more information.\par
\par
\tab    Implementation Restrictions.\par
\par
\tab    Due to planned changes in P?S/8 Version 9A (but not yet\par
 \tab    implemented), the cross-reference output is currently \tab\tab\tab    written to the system device logical unit 7 starting at block\par
\tab    0000; all prior contents of this device will be overwritten.\par
\tab    No additional output of cross-reference data is available.\par
 \tab    Furthermore, no final processing is provided in the current\par
\tab    release.\par
\par
\tab    Once the cross-reference output programming is upgraded to\par
\tab    allow allocation of extended-length files via a user-settable\par
\tab    configuration table, this restriction will be eliminated.\par
\tab    Significantly larger programs will be handled than presently\par
\tab    possible on appropriate configurations (such as RK8E/RK05-\tab\tab    based systems) due to the availability of multiple extended-\par
\tab    length files to store reference data during the assembly.\par
\par
\tab    As of this writing, the processing of the cross-reference\par
 \tab    output is not fully implemented pending the changes described\par
\tab    above with regard to the cross-reference output data; however, \tab    both statement numbering and creation of reference data are\par
\tab    functional.  Logical unit 7 must be available as described\par
\tab    above.\par
\par
\tab    Note: PAL8 does not directly support cross-reference output.\par
\tab    As an alternative, OS/8 listing files are reparsed by OS/8\par
\tab    \i CREF\i0  (if enabled) as a post-processing utility.  There are\par
\tab    several disadvantages to this method:\par
\par
\tab a) The listing output of PAL8 is not a statement-by-statement \tab\tab    listing file; statements hidden by the use of the XLIST\par
\tab    directive (and related techniques) do not appear in the\par
\tab    listing file.  As such, the cross-referenced listing output\par
\tab    is potentially incomplete; statement numbers are inherently\par
\tab    inaccurate and untrustworthy.\par
\par
\tab    By way of contrast, P?S/8 PAL statement numbering is\par
\tab    compatible with PAL10, which correctly shows statement numbers\par
 \tab    revealing the presence of any hidden statements.\page\tab b) A valid technique for including documentation within PAL\par
\tab    source code is to use deliberately failing conditional\par
\tab    assembly on a section of commentary as large as an entire\par
 \tab    printable page of the listing.  Content examples include\par
\tab    details regarding program operation and \ul\i banner\i0  \i pages\ulnone\i0  of large\par
\tab    character outlines created from a series of smaller\par
\tab    characters; the nature of this form of output is limited only\par
\tab    by the programmer's creativity.\par
\par
\tab    Unfortunately, CREF has no ability to discern this aspect of\par
\tab    a PAL8 listing file as commentary; as such, the commentary is\par
\tab    cross-referenced as if part of assembled code-generating\par
\tab    statements.  When banner pages are used, there can be\par
\tab    numerous erroneous junk references to symbols that merely\par
\tab    correspond to the individual characters used to build up the\par
\tab    larger character layouts; as such, the cross-reference output\par
\tab    can be cluttered with many superfluous references.  Since CREF\par
\tab    has limitations on overall symbol table size, this can have a\par
\tab    deleterious effect on the overall capability of CREF.  See c)\par
\tab    and d) below.\par
\par
\tab c) CREF internally reckons statement numbers as 13-bit integers\par
\tab    in the range of 1 through 8191 with an odd quirk: additional\par
\tab    statements wrap back to 4096 instead of 1.  As such, there\par
\tab    is inherent anomaly in the cross-reference output regarding\par
\tab    precise statement numbering of individual references.\par
\par
\tab    By way of contrast, P?S/8 PAL and PAL10 allow unique statement\par
\tab    numbering in the range of 1 through 99999.\par
\par
\tab d) CREF has limited overall capability.  If the entire cross-\par
\tab    reference cannot be accomplished in one pass, the program\par
\tab    aborts unless the /M option switch is set.  If the /M option\par
\tab    switch is enabled, CREF operates as a strictly-defined two-\par
\tab    pass operation which may (eventually) allow the process to\par
\tab    complete.  However, each \i half\i0  of the overall operation must\par
\tab    succeed to obtain a successful overall result.\par
\par
\tab    Note: A quirk of the two-pass method used is the insertion of\par
\tab    an extraneous \highlight7 <FF>\highlight0  in the output stream when the second pass\par
 \tab    begins.  (This is documented but not explained.)\par
\par
\tab    The split-point for this so-called \i mammoth\i0  mode is set to the\par
\tab    theoretical symbol \i LGNNNN\i0 .  References to all symbols that\par
\tab    sort lower than this value are scanned during the first pass\par
\tab    while ignoring references to symbols that sort higher.  The\par
\tab    second pass processes all symbols that sort higher than \i LGNNNN\i0  \tab    including all references to current-page and page zero\par
\tab    literals; the references processed in the first pass are\par
 \tab    ignored.  Each pass must scan the entire listing file.\page\tab    Because of the arbitrary nature of the split-point, it is \tab\tab    possible for the overall cross-reference to fail because the\par
 \tab    program symbols are not \i balanced\i0  sufficiently to conform to\par
\tab    this arbitrary scheme (for which there are no options).\par
\par
\tab e) Because of the inherently redundant nature of rescanning text\par
\tab    files attempting to obtain information about the assembly\par
\tab    without having access to the original symbol table and parsing\par
\tab    techniques, CREF operates very slowly.\par
\par
\tab    By way of contrast, assembler-based cross-reference\par
\tab    implementation has the advantage of only processing legitimate\par
\tab    references to symbols and literals; commentary is ignored.\par
\tab    Instead of rescanning entire listing files, the essential\par
\tab    cross-reference data is scanned as many times as necessary to\par
 \tab    process all references; there is no arbitrary static split-\par
\tab    point nor requirement of at most two passes on the far smaller\par
\tab    set of data.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Many large PAL programs cannot be completely processed\par
\tab    by CREF.  This includes (ironically) the source code of P?S/8\par
\tab    PAL.\par
\par
\ul 2.06 Literal and link options\par
\par
\ulnone Properly utilized, PDP-8 literal statements allow rapid implementation of working programs.  That said, certain compromises may arise:\line\line Often a PDP-8 programmer is concerned with producing the smallest possible coding for a given implementation.  Certain portions of operating systems have stringent coding requirements with regard to an extremely limited program space.  Often the expediency of using literals must be abandoned in favor of numerous tricks for which there is no analogue in other computer architectures:\line\line\tab a) Intrepid programmers can \cf8 create\cf0  created\cf3  (create)\cf0  needed literal values in\par
\tab    various ways.  The techniques generally use either the\par
\tab    contrived location of instructions to force generation of\par
\tab    needed constants or the use of seemingly superfluous OPR\par
\tab    statement conditions that can create fortuitous constants\par
\tab    regardless of code position.  In rare cases, multiple\par
\tab    contrivances are used in \cf8 concert\cf0  consort\cf3  (concert?)\cf0  to achieve even tighter \cf8 concert and consort are interesting words somewhat similar to each other in context.\par
\cf0\par
\tab    binary code.  The ability to perform extreme optimization\par
  \tab    techniques requires intimate knowledge of the PDP-8\par
\tab    instruction set in terms of operation code values, etc.\line\line\tab b) When the contrived literal values are achieved, the use of\par
\tab    generated literal statements must be abandoned where\par
\tab    applicable.  Thus, this extreme form of optimization is\par
 \tab    contrary to the convenience of using generated literals.\page\tab c) Purely for documentation purposes, some programmers use source\par
\tab    code statements that include the syntax of the equivalent\par
\tab    literal; however, that portion of the statement is part of an\par
\tab    extended comment.  This usage explains the intentions of the\par
\tab    programmer prior to the optimization.\line\line\tab d) In general, the use of literals discourages code space\par
\tab    optimization; as such, literals should be used if as\cf3  (as?)\cf8 this is correct as stated. I think I sent you e-mail on it\par
\cf0  and when\par
\tab    appropriate.  Many operating system components contain once-\tab\par
\tab    only initialization sections which are later repurposed as\par
\tab    buffers or table/list space.  It is totally appropriate to\par
\tab    include literal statements within such code; generally, there\par
\tab    is little to be gained using optimization techniques.\par
\par
\tab e) Beginning programmers are encouraged to learn the proper use\par
\tab    of literals just after mastering the general instruction set.\par
\tab    Code optimization is not a factor until the programmer is\par
\tab    quite experienced.  As required, programs can be (partially)\par
\tab    optimized by removing literal statements as appropriate once\par
\tab    the complexity of the project clearly demands optimization\par
\tab    after the basic structure has been achieved.\par
\par
\f1 Since PDP-8 code is generally efficient for most applications, a common technique is the use of literals during early development, perhaps in conjunction with conditional literals as described below.  Only when necessary, optimization techniques can be applied (while simultaneously removing literal statements).\par
\par
Many of the system components of P?S/8 do not use literals while other sections use literals in at least one of several contexts usually related to program initialization.  This includes the implementation of P?S/8 PAL itself.  Most conforming user-written programs use identical techniques during development.\par
\par
In P?S/8 PAL\cf3 ,\cf0  literals and/or generated links are enabled using a combination of option switches as follows:\par
\par
\f0\tab /Q Enable generation of current page and page zero literals.\par
 \tab    Link generation is disabled; attempts at off-page references\par
\tab    will be treated as errors.\par
\par
\tab /O Enable generation of current page and page zero literals.\par
\tab    Link generation is enabled; however, links will be flagged \tab\tab    as errors.\par
\par
\tab /E Reset the current page literal extent when leaving the latest\par
\tab    page.\cf3   Migraine interfering with understanding?, but don't understand "literal extent" here.\par
\par
\cf1 The literal extent is a definition/term of art.\line\line When you first start assembly on a page such as 0200-0377, let's say you use an instruction such as TAD (1000)\line\line This generates 1377 to access the last word on the page automatically, and also a table of constants that ENDS? with this 1000 needed so far.\line\line Assume then another instruction exists a few locations down to access a different constant such as TAD (2500).  This generates 1376 and lengths the table to include now the constant 2500 then 1000 starting at 0376-0377.  The more literals you use that are unique [literals are reused if identical to somewhat optimize the program] the longer the table becomes but it starts ever closer to the front of the page.  Also, the code itself tends to "grow" longer towards the end of the page.  When the code meets and then overlaps the latest literal which is at the lowest address on the page, the page has been exceeded.\line\line The relevant feature is mostly worthless but fully supported presumably to help beginners; I personally never use it, and I don't know anyone else that does, but this is the feature:\line\line\par
As you leave any of the 31 current page candidates, the extent of how far towards the front of the page is remembered for the purpose of LATER coming back to the same page to continue perhaps adding addtiional literals.  The saved LITERAL EXTENT allows this code being created on the revisitation of the page to be consistent with the literals already generated previously.  However, the actual literals have already been dumped and are not remembered, just the EXTENT of where they existed.\line\line Thus, during the assembly, revisiting one of the 31 decimal pages restores the literal extent address value to allow more literals in lower-still addresses, but were there to be a redundant value, it unfortunately cannot know thus that case would produce another literal with the same value.  It all works, but it is inefficient.\line\line So, when you LEAVE one of the current pages, the literal extent is saved in a table of extents for each current page possible.  When you revisit the page, the literal extent is restored.  When you go to a different current page, that page's literal extent is restored, and when you leave a page, the then-current literal extent is stored in the internal table entry for that current page, etc.\line\line This is sometimes referred to as "remembering" the literal extents and in both PAL8 ans P?S/8 PAL is the default action.\line\line Option switches in both assemblers TURNS OFF this "feature".\line\line The reason you need the FORGETTING is that code often APPEARS to be in the same as a previous current page programming, but in reality it is RELOCATED in any of a variety of ways.\line\line *1000\line\line RELOC 200\line\line Any code in this code is NOT really in 200, it is meant to be used as some form of overlay that WILL be located there by some unstated means and thus, this is really a different current page usage.\line\line Perhaps the second usage is written out to a tape block, but when read into 0200 later, this is what you want.\line\line Thus, a typical usage is in an assembly language program that has overlay sections read in later.  Both FOCAL and PAL have several such overlays and if literals are in effect, they have to be properly "forgotten" and not "remembered" etc.\line\line In future assemblers, I would in fact out of personal preference [and sanity] reverse the default.\line\line No one ever thought through WHY this should be defaulted to the seldom used "remember" setting, so the option switch gets described as turning off remembering or in OS/8 manuals simply "forgetting" literals, etc.\line\line Creating an operating system often is a matter of loading memory only once with the images of programs that are not in a useful place for later execution, this this is a prime area where the forget feature is needed. It's not exactly an overlay, but the principle of creating code NOW for execution in a different location LATER applies, etc.\line\line\cf0\par
\par
\tab    Note: The use of the /E option switch requires some\par
\tab    combination of the /Q and /O option switches to be\par
\tab    meaningful.\page Enabling literal generation and/or link generation requires three additional pages of allocated memory.  See Section 2.13 of this document for more information regarding memory allocation and related\par
issues.\par
\par
Using the /Q option switch in conjunction with the /O option switch will suppress error messages on generated links; however, the count of links generated is always included in the statistics page produced at the end of the assembly.\par
\par
Assembly listing lines containing generated links will be flagged with a \highlight7\f2 '\highlight0\f0  character placed to the right of the generated instruction value. This feature cannot be disabled in P?S/8 PAL, PAL8 or PAL10.\par
\par
Note: PAL8 and PAL10 are compatible with P?S/8 PAL with regard to all\par
aspects of the disposition of generated link statements; however, all features regarding literals in PAL8 cannot be disabled.  See below for detailed warnings regarding generated links that apply to all major PDP-8 assemblers.\par
\par
\ul 2.07 Limitations of generated links\ulnone\par
\par
In general, generated links should be completely avoided; the short version: \b Generated links do not work!\par
\par
\b0 While this simple caveat ought to be sufficient, a short study of the PDP-8 architecture reveals specific problems as follows:\par
\par
1) Link generation is an attempt to partially abstract the PDP-8\par
   architecture.\cf3   (I don't understand "partially abstracting the PDP-8 architecture -- can you point me to a reference?)\cf0  \cf1 You are reading too much into this.\line\line Abstraction is defined as any feature where the actual workings of the architecure is obfuscated into suggesting something else is present other than the real hardware.  This is a tiny thus "partial' abstraction because it is just on the edge of the problem.  It makes it seem that the very real problem of obeying paging architectural rules is whenever you feel like it becomes optional and/or unimportant, but it is a fiction because of course the real rules ALWAYS apply.  Notice that there are rabid supporters of insane amounts of obfuscation and I am firmly against all of that, but this is letting the door open a crack so to speak.\par
\cf0\par
\par
\par
 Unfortunately, this leads to sloppy programming\par
   techniques that (might) function while violating the hard-wired\par
   rules of the PDP-8 instruction set.\par
\par
   A generated link is an indirect memory reference used to modify a\par
   malformed attempt at a direct memory reference that cannot be\par
   achieved; this is accomplished by using an address literal created\par
   in a manner analogous to that used within literal statements.\par
\par
   The program specifies a direct memory reference that is impossible\par
   due to the violation of memory addressing rules; instead an\par
   indirect reference is automatically created by the assembler\par
   (assuming this feature is enabled).  Since this process has much\par
   in common with the creation of literals, all other aspects of\par
   literal generation applies.\par
\par
   The following assembled code fragment illustrates an attempt to\par
   violate the rules of the PDP-8 instruction set; the flawed\par
   statement is flagged as an error because link generation was not\par
   enabled.\par
\par
          *0200          *200            /TYPICAL START ON PAGE 1.\par
\par
   IR  0577  \par
   000200  1377          TAD     577     /THIS REALLY CAN'T WORK.\page    The attempted usage cannot be achieved since the operand is outside\par
   of the allowed address range (0200-0377 in this instance); location\par
   0577 is part of the following page (0400-0577).  For an example\par
   such as this, locations on any page from 2 (0400-0577) through 37\par
   (7600-7777) are equally inaccessible.  The instruction is flagged\par
   with the \highlight7 IR\highlight0  error message (Illegal Reference).\par
\par
   The next example is the identical code fragment assembled with link\par
   generation enabled.  There are no errors indicated, but the\par
   statement is flagged with \highlight7 '\highlight0  indicating link generation.\par
\par
          *0200          *200            /TYPICAL START ON PAGE 1.\par
\par
   000200  1777'         TAD     577     /THIS REALLY CAN'T WORK.\par
          *0377 \par
   000377  0577\par
\par
   The generated code is an indirect instruction in the range of 1600-\par
   1777 as a consequence of the modification; a direct instruction\par
   would be in the range of 1200-1377.  The code also creates an\par
   address constant; the table of literal/link addresses is dumped at\par
   the end of the assembly.\par
\par
2) If link generation is enabled, the same source code produces\par
   flagged output.  The instruction at 0200 is changed to \ul\i TAD I 377\ulnone\i0 .\par
   Additionally, location 0377 is generated containing 0577.  Ignoring\par
   for the moment the instruction is now an indirect reference to 0577\par
   using the pointer address 0377 as an intermediary, to a naive PDP-8\par
   programmer (unaware of the potential negative consequences), the\par
   intended logic is satisfied: the contents of location 0577 are\par
   added to the accumulator.\par
\par
Note: In these coding examples, symbolic references are deliberately not used to prevent clouding the issues.  Actual attempts at link generation are usually in symbolic form; had the code been located in an accessible location, no link would be required.  Links often come about when code overflows the intended page into the next page.  By using purely numerical statements, the results are completely clear.\par
\par
3) To the posited programmer, the program fragment \ul\i works\i0  \i per se\ulnone\i0 .\par
   Unfortunately, in real PDP-8 programming, considerations outside of\par
   this example tend to crop up; programs that \ul\i had worked\ulnone\i0  suddenly \ul\i no\par
\ulnone\i0    \ul\i longer work\ulnone\i0  and might also cause unavoidable assembly errors of a\par
   kind that merely enabling link generation cannot remedy.\par
\par
   Such a programmer might be tempted to use a location already\par
   inaccessible (due to being on another page from the instruction) as\par
   a pointer.  The assembler will properly mark this with the error\par
   message \highlight7 II\highlight0  (Illegal Indirect).  Since it takes a pointer to access\par
   indirectly the address of the pointer, it is not possible to use it\par
   indirectly.  Had the programmer obeyed the rules of the PDP-8\par
   instruction set, this would have come about without sudden\par
   surprise.\page    Before investigating the insidious nature of link generation\par
   failing to work when no error messages occur (just generated link\par
   flagging), it will be shown that certain forms of generated links\par
   actually work as intended, as long as the mechanism is not pushed\par
   to the point of impossibility.\par
\par
   In all cases, links involving \ul\i Direct JMP\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i Direct JMS\ulnone\i0  instructions\par
   properly function in the sense described above.  (Statements\par
   attempting link generation of the form \ul\i JMS I\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i JMP I\ulnone\i0  cannot\par
   function as explained above.)  If this were the only issue, lazy\par
   programmers might have a valid excuse to enable link generation.\par
\par
   Unfortunately, all of the other forms of link generation will fail\par
   depending on the exact nature of the running program; the assembler\par
   cannot have knowledge of the programmer's intentions in the dynamic\par
   sense.\par
\par
   Repeating the generated link example from above:\par
\par
          *0200          *200            /TYPICAL START ON PAGE 1.\par
\par
   000200  1777'         TAD     577     /THIS REALLY CAN'T WORK.\par
          *0377 \par
   000377  0577\par
\par
   A possible use of the example program includes manual start from\par
   the computer front panel; this allows setting the data field (DF)\par
   to a non-zero value in the range of 1 through 7.\par
\par
   When started this way, the program does not accomplish the intended\par
   function; the contents of location 0577 in the data field are added\par
   to the accumulator.\par
\par
   In general, PDP-8 programs are written for the 32K address space.\par
   The data field is often used as an internal convention of the\par
   program logic.  When the program uses direct references (as the\par
   nominal statements attempt to do in the example), the data field\par
   plays no role in the logic of the program.  Programmers who never\par
   enable link generation are certain their programs are designed to\par
   work as expected (as long as no other statements are flagged with\par
   errors).\par
\par
   Note: Lack of generated links is no guarantee there are no logic\par
   errors in the program; however, at least this area of bad design is\par
   not present.\par
\par
4) As soon as extended memory factors creep into the logic of a\par
   program dependent on link generation, insidious failures start to\par
   occur.  The programmer who never learned the discipline required to\par
   avoid this programming pitfall is now faced with a nearly insoluble\par
   dilemma.  The only remedy to this situation is to \ul\i bite the bullet\par
\ulnone\i0    so to speak, eliminating all generated links as a first step\par
   towards debugging the program; by preventing the attendant error\par
   messages much of the problem will be eliminated.\page    Note: Cleaning up after link generation might involve a large\par
   expenditure of effort all at once; had links never been used, the\par
   work necessary to eliminate assembly errors might have been\par
   accomplished in small increments.\par
\par
In short, link generation correctly functions only if \ul\i Direct JMP\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i Direct JMS\ulnone\i0  instructions are involved.  Link generation fails to \i work\i0  if any form of \ul\i AND, TAD, ISZ or DCA\ulnone\i0  instructions are involved.  All forms of indirect usage attempts always fail.\par
\par
