\f0 P?S/8 PAL supports the two general forms of literal generation along with a unique third form that can shorten program development time.\par
\par
\tab a) Current page literals - The operand address is located on the\par
\tab    current page of the program; the address is assigned by the\par
\tab    assembler starting at relative location 177.  Additional\par
 \tab    literals are created in reverse order; a second current page\par
 \tab    literal is placed at relative location 176.\par
\par
\tab    The maximum number of current page literals allowed is 63\par
\tab    (decimal) in the relative range of 101-177.  When a statement\par
\tab    contains a current page literal, the current page literal\par
\tab    table is searched for a matching value.  If successful, the\par
\tab    existing literal address is used.  New values create\par
\tab    additional current page literals.  When either too many\par
\tab    current page literals are attempted or statements on the\par
\tab    current page overflow into the literal table, a \highlight7 PE\highlight0  (Page\par
\tab    Exceeded) error message is displayed.\par
\par
\tab    A typical current page literal usage follows:\par
\par
\tab    \tab    \tab    TAD\tab (3)\tab\tab /ADD 0003 TO THE ACCUMULATOR.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The trailing right parenthesis character is optional\par
\tab    unless nested expressions are used.\par
\par
\tab b) Page Zero literals - The operand address is located on Page\par
 \tab    Zero; the address is assigned by the assembler starting at\par
 \tab    location 0177.  Additional literals are created in reverse\par
\tab    order; a second Page Zero literal is placed at location 0176.\par
\par
\tab    The maximum number of Page Zero literals is 112 (decimal) in\par
\tab    the range of 0020-0177.  (Locations lower than 0020 are\par
\tab    disallowed due to the special auto-index characteristics of\par
\tab    locations 0010-0017 when indirectly referenced.)\par
\par
\tab    When a statement contains a Page Zero literal, the Page Zero\par
 \tab    literal table is searched for a matching value.  If\par
\tab    successful, the existing literal address is used.  New values\par
\tab    create additional Page Zero literals.  When either too many\par
\tab    Page Zero literals are attempted or statements on Page Zero\par
\tab    overflow into the literal table, a \highlight7 ZE\highlight0  (Page Zero Exceeded)\par
\tab    error message is displayed.\par
\par
\tab    A typical Page Zero literal usage follows:\par
\par
\tab    \tab    \tab    TAD\tab [3]\tab\tab /ADD 0003 TO THE ACCUMULATOR.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The trailing right square bracket character is optional\par
\tab    unless nested expressions are used.\page\tab c) Conditional literals - The operand address is either on the\par
 \tab    current page or on Page Zero depending upon specific\par
\tab    circumstances.  If a matching Page Zero literal value exists,\par
\tab    it will be used.  Failing a Page Zero match, If a matching\par
\tab    current page literal exists, it will be used.  Failing both a\par
\tab    Page Zero match and a current page match, a new current page\par
\tab    literal is created (subject to the possibility of a \highlight7 PE\highlight0  error\par
\tab    if the current page overflows).\par
\par
\tab    A typical conditional literal usage follows:\par
\par
\tab    \tab    \tab    TAD\tab #3\tab\tab /ADD 0003 TO THE ACCUMULATOR.\par
\par
\tab    The actual code generated is dependent on the literal pools in\par
\tab    effect at the point in the assembly the usage is encountered.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Since there is no corresponding closing character, it is\par
\tab    impossible to create nested conditional literal expressions.\par
\par
\tab    A common technique employed in well-developed programs is the\par
\tab    use of source code libraries of commonly used subroutines.\par
\tab    It is desirable to use Page Zero literals where appropriate.\par
\tab    However, Page Zero literals should not be used if there are\par
\tab    only singular references.  By using conditional literals, more\par
\tab    effective management of memory is achieved without the need to\par
\tab    hand-optimize the library source code statements containing\par
\tab    literals.\par
\par
\tab    Additional information about conditional literals and related\par
\tab    subjects can be found in Appendix C of this document.\par
\par
\tab    Note: This feature is not supported by PAL10 or PAL8.\par
\par
\ul 2.09 Literal extent issues\ulnone\par
\par
In general, all literals associated with any current page should be defined in proximate order to the code statements referencing them.  This facilitates locating any potential matches to avoid new literals (if possible).\par
\par
Leaving the present page, the existing literals are dumped to finish the intent and requirements of all source code statements defined so far.\par
\par
If that page is ever revisited during the assembly, it is possible to define additional literals on the page because the literal extent value formerly in effect was saved and will be restored.  However, since the actual former literals have been dumped, all new literals will only be able to search for a match within the newly-created literal pool.\par
\par
Note: This feature, while enabled by default, is hardly ever used.  It is supported primarily to maintain compatibility with the literal generation features of PAL10 and PAL8.\page If the /E option switch is used, the literal extent when leaving a current page is totally reset; no history of previous literals on any current page is retained.\par
\par
Note: The use of the /E option switch does not impact page zero literals (which are completely reset upon either the end of the assembly or the use of the FIELD directive).  In certain PDP-8 documents, this is referred to as \i forgetting\i0  current page literals.\par
\par
Resetting literal extents is useful when defining overlays.  The overlay code must be created in the context of another section of the assembly which presumably will be replaced in memory by the overlay code; however, the overlay code must maintain its own current page literals as required.\par
\par
while there are many techniques associated with the deployment of overlays, the /E option switch is always required to allow overlays to maintain their own current page literal pools.\par
\par
Note: Future implementations of P?S/8 PAL (or a new assembler for the P?S/8 \i SHELL\i0  based on P?S/8 PAL tentatively to be known as \i XPAL\i0 ) will likely invert the default condition regarding literal extent retention due to disuse of the current default; as a practical matter, sections of program code that revisit formerly used pages are always part of an overlay structure.  There are no justifiable reasons to deliberately separate small program sections on the same code page.\par
\par
\ul 2.10 \f2 Dual \f0 a\f2 ssembly \f0 m\f2 ode \f0 o\f2 ptions\ulnone\par
