\ul 2.41 Text directive issues\ulnone\par
\par
The PAL language defines the TEXT directive which is used with a string argument.  The string consists of an indefinite number of printable characters preceded by a delimiter character which cannot be included in the generated text; the string ends with a second usage of the delimiter character.\page Note: P?S/8 PAL does not require the trailing delimiter character unless there are multiple statements on the same line.\par
\par
Early implementations of the TEXT directive restricted the delimiter character to \highlight7 "\highlight0 , \highlight7 '\highlight0  and in some cases \highlight7 @\highlight0  (which is not supported by P?S/8 PAL and other assemblers).  The use of \highlight7 "\highlight0  or \highlight7 '\highlight0  as text delimiters is recommended for maximum compatibility with other PAL assemblers; P?S/8 PAL and PAL8 support an extension allowing any printing character not\par
contained within the text string to be used as the delimiter character.\par
\par
All usage of the TEXT directive creates strings of six-bit ASCII text\par
characters packed two per 12-bit word.  If the count of characters in the string is an odd number, the last 12-bit word has 00 in the low-order six bits which can act as a string delimiter.\par
\par
Since the most likely usage of the TEXT directive is to generate strings used with printing subroutines, the default action for string arguments with even character counts is to generate an additional\par
12-bit word of 0000 to ensure the presence of a string delimiter.\par
\par
TEXT directive usage is not limited to string creation as described\par
above; some alternate schemes use internal printing characters as delimiters or other control purposes.  Within the P?S/8 keyboard  monitor coding, explicit string length arguments are used in some instances; in other cases the string length is an implicit constant value.  The 00 word plays no role in any of these cases; creation of an extra 12-bit word of 0000 is undesirable.\cf3   Added an extra single space before "Within" above.\line\line\cf1 OK.  This is obviously an artifact of repagination from Jarte.\cf0\par
\par
Note: Early PDP-8 programs such as \ul\i FOCAL, 1969\ulnone\i0  were developed on systems incapable of modifying the output of the TEXT directive; as such, the source code includes statements to backup the assembler current location counter to allow the desired contents which differs from the automatically generated 0000 value.  Ironically, this form of source-level workaround is incompatible with the /J option switch (which eliminated the problem several years after the program was\par
developed).\par
\par
As documented above, setting the /J option switch prevents the creation of the extra 12-bit word of 0000 on even count text strings.  It is strongly recommended the disposition of the /J option switch be documented at the beginning of all source code; in many cases, this may be the only departure from assembler default settings and could easily be overlooked.\par
\par
\ul 2.42 Other text-related issues\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL supports the SIXBIT directive which takes the same general form as the TEXT directive.  The only difference is the internal six-bit code used is not the PDP-8 standard six-bit subset of the ASCII character set; instead, the alternate definition of six-bit text as used on DEC 18-bit and 36-bit computers applies.\page The basic difference between the two character sets is the highest of the six bits used is inverted.  Input processing of seven-bit ASCII characters to six-bit code is easier using the PDP-8 standard while output processing of six-bit code to seven-bit ASCII characters is easier using the alternative standard.  The PDP-8 standard was chosen largely because there are often multiple input routines while output is generally performed by a single output routine.\par
\par
Note: PAL8 does not support the SIXBIT directive.\par
\par
The DEVICE and FILENA\{ME\} directives are supported by P?S/8 PAL and PAL8.  These are special-purpose text directives to create a four-character and eight-character text string respectively.  Early OS/8 programs required the use of the /J option switch (or its equivalent in PAL8 which is the /F option switch); this allows creation of the intended string arguments while preventing compatibility with the usual string printing applications, which require even-length string termination.  With the availability of the DEVICE and FILENA\{ME\} directives, the normal flexibility of text string creation is restored.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 supports the FILENA\{ME\} directive for compatibility purposes only.  Future P?S/8 systems will support the P?S/8 SHELL with an extended assembler based on P?S/8 PAL to be known as \i XPAL\i0 .  XPAL will support an option to extend the FILENA\{ME\} directive to support SHELL directory file names.\cf3   Shouldn't XPAL always be italicised?\line\line\cf1 This was discussed above; the answere will be consistent with that decision, etc.\cf0\par
\par
Certain user-modified assemblers exist that support the \i TEXTZ\i0  directive which forces the extra 12-bit word of 0000 even if the /J option switch (or equivalent) is in effect.  Future versions of P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  may support the TEXTZ directive and the \i SIXBTZ\i0  directive (using the alternate six-bit text code).\par
\par
Note: As of PAL8 Version B0 (the last version released by DEC), the TEXTZ directive is not supported.\par
\par
Most PAL assemblers (including P?S/8 PAL and PAL8) support the \highlight7 "\highlight0  operator in expressions.  The next character (which cannot be \highlight7 <HT>\highlight0 ) is used to form the seven-bit ASCII value of the stated character; the mark parity bit is set.\par
\par
A proposed \i ASCII\i0  directive would take a string argument much like the TEXT directive to create a string of seven-bit ASCII characters.  The /J option switch would prevent a trailing 12-bit word of 0000.\par
\par
An additional \i ASCIIZ\i0  directive could be implemented to force the creation of the trailing 12-bit word of 0000 regardless of the setting of the /J option switch.\par
\par
Note: An additional issue is whether to have an option to prevent the mark parity bit within the text string as arithmetic operations are not possible on individual string characters.\page Even without any form of \i ASCII\i0  (or similar) directive, it is currently possible to create \ul seven-bit ASCII\ulnone  text strings with any of the discussed variations using the \highlight7 "\highlight0  operator.  When used alone, each character in the string will have the mark parity set; string delimiter characters can be added if desired, such as ending the string with a 12-bit word of 0000 or making the last character of the string negative (in the 12-bit sense).  By adding an additional element in each expression, it is possible to strip off the mark parity bit as follows:\par
\par
HLOWRLD,"H&177;"E&177;"L&177;"L&177;"O&177;" &177\tab /HELLO WORLD MESSAGE\par
        "W&177;"O&177;"R&177;"L&177;"D&177!4000\tab /NEGATIVE ENDS TEXT.\par
\par
An unnamed additional text directive has been proposed to create packed seven-bit ASCII text strings following the \ul\i three for two\i0  \ulnone packing convention as used in OS/8 text files (and the P?S/8 SHELL).  Because of alignment restrictions, additional null eight-bit bytes will be required, regardless of the issue regarding the mark parity bit.  If the string is meant to be an image of an actual file section, there must also be a mechanism to append a trailing \highlight7 Control-Z\highlight0  character into the string.\par
\par
Note: The issue of a \ul three for two\ulnone  directive will likely be taken up in the XPAL assembler which will support upper-case/lower-case input files.  A \i PARITY\i0  directive with appropriate argument support can be implemented to handle all mark parity bit (and perhaps\cf3  the?\cf0  \highlight7 Control-Z\highlight0  character) issues raised above.\par
