\ul 2.61 Issues regarding the DTORG directive\ulnone\par
\par
The OS/8 implementation of the DTORG directive is deliberately avoided in P?S/8 PAL.  A proposed solution to the underlying problem is presented below which will allow the DTORG directive to be useful to P?S/8 system generation in future releases of P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
\ul 2.62 PAL8 implementation of DTORG\ulnone\par
\par
As currently supported in PAL8, the DTORG directive is completely compatible with PAL10.  The DTORG directive is meant to create (128 words/block) absolute block references within the binary output to allow a special-purpose loader (a component of the DEC \i Typeset-8\i0  group\par
system programming software) to write Typeset-8 system blocks on DECtape (and perhaps other devices).\par
\par
The general function is to specify a numbered storage block on a TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape followed by the (up to) 128 words to be written to the specified block.\par
\par
The origin in memory is set prior to the DTORG directive; however, it is generally ignored because no form of standard loader (such as the BIN paper-tape binary loader) is supported.  The origin is used to define the upper limit in memory of the (up to) 128 data words that follow; once an entire PDP-8 page has been defined (placing the current location counter on the next PDP-8 page), the intended block contents are finalized.\cf3   Should BIN always be italicised?\par
\par
\cf1 This was discussed elsewhere.\cf0\line\line Typeset-8 programs create a series of these small sections; each is loaded by a special-purpose Typeset-8 generation utility which writes the data to the specified DECtape (or other device) logical block.  However, there are serious issues:\par
\par
\tab a) Each section written to a targeted DECtape block is taken\par
\tab    from a lengthy punched binary paper-tape created at an OS/8\par
 \tab    development site; the tape invariably is the latest release\par
 \tab    of a major section of the Typeset operating system (or one\par
 \tab    of its utilities).  In-house \cf3 (i.e., at DEC?)\par
\cf1 Of course, no one ever got Typeset-8 source code, just customized working systems tailored to EACH customer's needs.\line\cf3\par
\par
 \cf0 Typeset-8 machines generally\par
\tab    do not include high-speed readers; these systems invariably\par
\tab    have Teletype Model 35 ASR terminals to read in the binary\par
\tab    data.\page\tab b) There is no form of checksum protection associated with the\par
 \tab    written data.  Since the binary paper-tapes are generally\par
 \tab    quite lengthy, this is patently foolhardy.\par
\par
\tab    By the nature of binary paper-tape, it is expected that\par
\tab    reading in long paper-tapes will occasionally result in read\par
\tab    errors.  This is why the BIN loader format includes checksum\par
 \tab    protection frames.  \par
\par
\tab    Note: The RIM loader, which lacks a checksum mechanism, is\par
\tab    only used to load short binary programs such as the BIN\par
 \tab    loader, which in turn can load in significant binary program\par
\tab    data with the confidence provided by passing an overall\par
\tab    checksum test.  It is unreasonable to expect to use the RIM\par
\tab    loader for lengthy binary files; the analogy between RIM\par
\tab    binary paper-tape loading and Typeset-8 binary paper-tape\par
\tab    loading is troubling.\par
\par
\tab c) Despite being created by PAL8 in OS/8 standard binary format\par
\tab    (as a .BN file), the binary output violates the OS/8 binary\par
\tab    file standard; the output of a program containing instances\par
\tab    of the DTORG directive cannot be loaded successfully by OS/8\par
\tab    \i ABSLDR\i0 .  Attempts to read in the punched binary paper-tape\par
\tab    will always end in a checksum error, even if an error-free\par
\tab    read was performed.  This is because the implementation of the\par
\tab    DTORG directive usurps the underlying BIN format that OS/8\par
\tab    converts into \ul\i three-for-two\ulnone\i0  format OS/8 records (384 frames\par
\tab    per logical record).\cf3   Is "\ul\i three-for-two\ulnone\i0 " defined anywhere related to this document?\page\cf0\page\cf1 I suppose it should be defined somewhere, but I am not sure.\line\line The format describes that fact that three 8-bit bytes are stored in two adjacent 12-bit words as follows explicitly.\line\line 1) This goes in the low-order 8 bits of the first 12-bit word.\line\line 2) This goes in the low-order 8 bits of the second word.\line\line 3) This byte is spit.  The high-order four become the 4 highmost bits in the first 12-bit word.  Similarly, the low-four go into the high 4-bits of the second 12-bit word.\line\line This is the substance of what the COROUTINE.PAL program does, returns the bytes in the original order by unpacking them from 3-for-2 into one at a time in the proper order.\line\line An entire buffer of them is done as needed.  The subroutine being called advances to return the next one in the proper order, etc.\line\line The example is cut out of Kermit-12 where a bit more coding makes it into the practical file unpacker as used in OS/8 and the SHELL text files with the exception of a more rigid definition of the 8th highest bit in any byte.\line\line\line\par
\par
\par
