\ul 2.63 Proposed remedies to the DTORG problem\ulnone\par
\par
Apparently due to internal DEC politics, no attempt was made to remedy this problem by educating the Typeset-8 programmers regarding their naive design; various well-known mechanisms can easily solve the problem as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) Instead of implementing the DTORG directive as a violation \tab\tab    of the internal binary format of BIN utilities and OS/8, the\par
 \tab    binary data can be patterned after a variety of utility\par
\tab    formats such as certain details of P?S/8 non-system device\par
 \tab    handlers and OS/8 device handlers (as required by OS/8 \i BUILD\i0 ).\par
\par
\tab    An arbitrary series of origin settings can be used to indicate\par
\tab    the intention of DTORG directive statements as follows:\par
\par
\tab    DTORG  1234               /DESIGNATE BLOCK 1234 FOR OUTPUT.\page\tab    This DTORG usage would create the equivalent of the following\par
 \tab    binary output:\par
\par
        PREV=  .                  /NEED THE PREVIOUS ORIGIN SETTING.\par
\par
        *7777;*1234;*PREV         /OUTPUT FLAG ORIGIN FOLLOWED BY\par
                                  /THE DTORG BLOCK NUMBER FOLLOWED\par
                                  /BY THE PREVIOUS ORIGIN.\par
\par
\tab    A special-purpose loader is still required to create Typset-8\par
\tab    system blocks in the proposed format; however, the binary data\par
\tab    created is compatible with all loading mechanisms; a lengthy\par
\tab    binary paper-tape punched out and then read on a Teletype\par
\tab    Model 35 ASR read station could be known to have passed the\par
\tab    checksum test.  Afterwards, all designated blocks can be\par
\tab    written out at once.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The Typeset-8 system runs on 4K machines.  The binary\par
\tab    paper tapes used would tend to be somewhat longer than the\par
\tab    binary paper-tape for \ul\i FOCAL, 1969\ulnone\i0  as a representative example\par
\tab    of comparable size.\cf3   Will the average reader/user know the length of \ul\i FOCAL, 1969\ulnone\i0 ?\par
\par
\cf1 You can say it is typically over 20 minutes.  Not the absoluete longest, but pretty hefty and a common practical example.  The Typeset-8 tapes would likely be up to even a bit longer, but could be shorter if only performing a patch. But building a Typeset-8 system from scratch undoubtedly is much longer in larger sections and a real lot of them, etc.\line\line\cf0\par
\par
\tab b) The example remedy represents the most expedient method to\par
\tab    solve the DTORG problem.  More elaborate schemes could include\par
\tab    additional origin settings, perhaps following the last two\par
\tab    data-related origins as shown in the example.  An overall\par
\tab    mini-checksum for the entire string of origin settings could\par
\tab    be created if desired.\par
\par
\tab    Regardless of implementation details, the entire problem of\par
 \tab    supporting incompatible binary files would be eliminated.  All\par
\tab    binary files can be used with all available binary format\par
\tab    utilities.  After punching the lengthy paper-tape, it could be\par
\tab    read back using the high-speed paper-tape reader.  The\par
\tab    resulting file would not only have to pass the standard\par
\tab    checksum test, but additionally could be directly compared\par
\tab    to the original binary output file to completely confirm the\par
\tab    validity of the paper-tape before bring\cf3 ing\cf0  the tape to the\par
\tab    Typeset-8 back-room facility for the usual purpose of\par
\tab    generating Typeset-8 system components.\par
\par
\tab c) Without having to support \cf3 the? or an? \cf1 an \cf0 incompatible binary format, the need\par
\tab    to store paper-tape binary frames becomes superfluous.  OS/8\par
\tab    could have easily been developed using a more efficient\par
\tab    internal file format similar to P?S/8 and the \ul\i R-L Monitor\ulnone\i0\par
\tab    \ul\i System\ulnone\i0 .\par
\par
\tab    It is well known \cf1 to the author of this document (cjl) and certain original staff programmers  \cf0\par
\par
 \cf3 to CJL & the OS/8 Programmers\par
\par
 \cf0 that DEC managers perverted certain aspects\par
 \tab    of the demo project that was eventually to become OS/8.  Their\par
