\par
\tab\tab The PQS directive is a unique feature of P?S/8 PAL.  It can \tab\tab be used in conditional assembly statements to allow special-\tab\tab purpose programming only supported by P?S/8 PAL; alternate \tab\tab\tab statements can attempt to provide compatibility with other \tab\tab\tab assemblers.  Unfortunately, certain powerful features of\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 PAL are not available in other assemblers; crude \tab\tab\tab alternatives \cf3 in other assemblers? \cf0 have been used to provide a functional, albeit \tab\tab inferior, result.\cf3   Have you done (some of) these alternatives?\par
\par
\cf1 All of the relevant assemblers have enough features to do a poor job that can create the binary correctly, but it is "ugly" or tedious or worse or all of the above.  A simple have/have not situation.  I can't help it that PAL8 as a mediocre assembler, and sometimes it is really a PITA when I have features available to do the immediate syntactical issue correcyly.  Also, there are situations where PAL10 and P?S/8 PAL BOTH have the right feature, but PAL8 lacks it!\line\line Sometimes this is solved by using a series of progressive equate statements that get you in the end what you needed.  But since PAL8 doesn't handle unary operators correctly [such as trying to put a - sign in front of a calculation to get the negated version of what calculation follows, AND it is part of the language spec AND both PAL10 and P?S/8 PAL do it correctly, I still have to deal with PAL8 as the "weak sister".  Thus, in the variant that PAL8 can handle, that requires an extra statement.  One to get the positive form at all, and then another that puts the minus sine in front of a symbol and not a more complicated expression [It might be MODESTLY more complicated].  In any case, an assembler is supposed to help develop source code, but in too many cases, you have to instead help PAL8 to get the results you want resorting to needless tedious and ugly intermediate results that are unnecessary in all other cases.  Fortunately, these cases do not come up a lot, but when they do, it is understandably annoying.\line\line Thus, a technique is to do this:\line\line Create two alternate litte sections of code.  Use conditional assembly such that if PQS is defined then do it the non-PAL8 way, and if it is undefined, then set it to 0.  That would be protected against in P?S/8 PAL because you cannot redefine a directive.  You can use FIELD, but you CANNOT use FIELD=1234, that makes no sense and thus isn't allowed in any assembler, but only P?S/8 PAL has PQS to be similarly protected, etc.\line\line So, you have statements like IFNDEF PQS <PQS= 0>\line\line If this is P?S/8 PAL, that is flushes as a failing conditional as intended.  And If say PAL8, then it executes as a non-failing conditional, etc.  Thus, PQS becomes 0000 OR it does not.\line\line Then, later in one of these situations.\line\line You have IFNZRO PQS <do stuff the elegant P?S/8 PAL way>\line\line and you also have IFZERO PQS <do it the ugly way>\line\line To make this cosmetically work, generos use of the XLIST directive makes it all look like none of it is there other than the original intended P?S/8 PAL original one-line definition or whatever we were doing, and if PAL8, then you get at least two lines to handle it, etc.\line\line Thus, as a programmer, to avoid overflow pages, I have to do all of the above and make sure the printed page has at least an extra line capability yet in P?S/8 PAL it is never going to be used, just so that if PAL8 then it could fill the page.\line\line Now all of this is over just one small theoretical yet practical problem that PAL8 causes.  It is a giant hill of annoyances in practical programming.  As such, some programs are filled with these structures just to get around the damn limitatons of PAL8, etc.\line\line This is at the heart of why I have to "cut the cord" and never again guarantee that PAL8 can produce correct output, screw it and never fix these things again, etc.  But I cannot until I am 100% ready to do so, and that's not yet, etc.\line\line Fortunately, in P?S/8 source code development, I have trained myself to generally avoid the problem wherever I can, so I am essentially compromising all the time, and that includes the constant having to check that comments are not too wide so they get truncated, even though in P?S/8 I can program for a 132 column printer if I wish to, but instead each line has to fit in either 86 columns of course code, but if it is a statement with a comment, you get 88 columns in those cases.  Yes, I have a two-stage template I put into the end of the source file and then drag a copy up occasionally to make sure that I get it just at the upper limit of what PAL8 CAN do.  Of course this is not a conditional fixable workaround, it is just a constant battle and I then have to do multiple line comments because I cannot fit what I want on one line for the want of sometims 12 extra characters!  That is not fixable, the only way is to cut the cord, etc.\line\line Thus, bottom line,the PQS directive helps in the occasional annoyance, but I ALWAYS have to make the "source margins" match the stupid PAL8 limitations.\line\line And this is right out of old 80 column punch card mentality.  Today's C programmers create source lines that go WAY to the right [and typically with NO COMMENTS!]\line\line All I want is the "right" to use the line printer hardware as my limit.  The DaVince package simulates it 100% so it is still the practical limit, and P?S?8 can actually go WIDER if anyone wanted to!!\par
\line PR Newswire paid me to make their source code PAL8 compatible at the expense of losing optimization if the program were seriously developed a lot further.  They insisted, so I took their money wasting my time, etc.  A lot of really ugly stuff to avoid using conditional literals, a seriously useful feature I invented, thus only in P?S/8 PAL.\line\line I can't help it if people "aim low" and want to work with a needlessly backward product.  I had licensed them P?S/8 for RK8E, etc. but they wanted to the absurd "what if I got run over by a truck?" scenario covered as well, on the theory that OS/8 had a handbook, when in fact the relevant section on PAL8 is really quite meager, and my "HELP" document for P?S/8 PAL provides about as much relevant info. The rest is a litte programming advise and examples, but if you are not a PDP-8 programmer, that is of little use to you anyway, and to an actual one, just skip that crap and maybe just look up the switches used.  I listed then in the beginning of the source code for both PAL8 and P?S/8 PAL.  There is no reasoning with stupid managers, but I am blameless in that, and it was rather stupid creating symbols for where the conditional literals created optimizations, and that becomes static ruining the whole purpose of the feature!\line\line Those are the three most ugly aspects of being PAL8 compatible.  The rest is acceptable, but not great, and remember it is MUCH SLOWER as well!\line\line Since I use PEPS to do source code editing, that whole burden is lifted for me.  The remaining stuff is fixing the XREF stuff in the next release when there are THE extended-length files as discussed and then implement using them and writing the printout part.  [It is the easiest to do, but I never got around to it, and I knew forcing it to use raw output to logical unit 7 is not really a good idea.  The reason I got away with it then was that this was a two RK05 system and it was configured that P?S/8 units 0-3 were on one pack, and 4-7 on the other.  But as you know, not the best is one pack is one logical unit, and there may only be 0-3 !  But they are really big, etc.  Thus, this is not practical unless and until you can specify which logical units THE extended-length file can be specified, and thus, unit 7 not even existing becomes fine, etc. And this is all of course BEFORE the SHELL, etc.  I want a fully functional P?S/8 PAL before I start creating the SHELL, and then eventually create a PALX or XPAL [which sounds better] based on P?S/8 PAL but for the SHELL environment that is even better, etc.]\line\line That and a new way of generating the system, and for that, I need to make a minor fix to P?S/8 PAL's handling of DTORG, which is trivial.  But depending on timing, I need to "bail out" OS/8 but not actually use PAL8! That's one of the reasons to write BINCON, and the other is that as long as PAL8 IS in the picture, then the defective DTORG output is corrected by BINCON, and then the corrected binary could be sent back to OS/8 with the problem corrected.  When that happens, then OS/8 can actually load the binary file "created" by PAL8 [because that is then not really how it got there! It took a round-trip to P?s/8 to "repair" the binary file and remove the Typeset-8 travesty, etc.\line\line I know sometimes it is hard to follow all of this, but it is crystal clear in my mind, and perhaps Bob Armstrong can keep up with the logistics if explained incrementally and defines the "road map" of where it has to go and in what order, etc.\line\line I need to be the software manager of six people like me :-) .\par
