SIXBIT \i text-string\i0\par
\par
\tab\tab The SIXBIT directive creates 12-bit words packed with six-\tab\tab\tab bit characters taken from the \i text-string\i0  (other than \tab\tab\tab\tab delimiter characters).  The encoding is six-bit ASCII text \tab\tab\tab as used with similar directives in assemblers for DEC 18-bit \tab\tab and 36-bit systems (which is not the PDP-8 standard).\cf3   Many/most users will no virtually nothing about these 18- & 36-bit systems, I think.  I certainly don't.\par
\par
\cf1 Before ASCII was defined, pretty much no viable character set in the industry defined upper/lower case [with the exception of TTS code, but that is too tied up in typesetting to be of general usage outside of that narrow industry.  5-level Baudot tapes get 6-bits worth of characters [almost] by defining a shift function up or down. Similarly, TTY code has a shift to get to 7-bit, but that's not a UNIQUE code per character system.  ASCII became the one that mattered forever even now, etc.\line\line All languages prior to C were NEVER lower-case languages at all!  There is a nit-pick argument about where comments should be allowed in lower-case, but in any case, PAL8 does support that, and P?S/8 PAL cannot because the character set of the files is sixbit, same as DIAL to within a couple of quirks.  [Remember, that Jack Burness wrote DIAL, and started on R-L as well, including helping to become actual in that famous 4 month period.\line\line Simply put, no one really wants lower case, but new people are obsessed with it out of laziness.\line\line Regardless, in a world where six-bit ASCII is the dominant form, meaning EVERYTHING from all the other DEC machines and Fortan and BASIC, etc. and the various assemblers, the files are completely definable using six-bit, etc. Note that P?S/8 uses horizontal tab and supports EJECT as the <FF> the same as DIAL and also PAL10 and even PAL8, that is why it is no problem to convert OS/8 assembly source to and from P?S/8 files, etc.\line\line Thus, silly comment issues aside, sixbit rules.\line\line That said, there is a them versus us problem.\line\line You can encode characters from full 7-bit ASCII one of two ways.\line\line 1) It is either trivial or only slightly more complicated to either input or output.\line\line 2) There is no net overhead when considering the sum of coding both input and output.\line\line 3) The 18-bit and 36-bit machines opted to use the easier to print sixbit,  but the PDP-8 opted for the easier to make sense of by reading and also input easier sixbit, which for the assembler syntax is known as TEXT while the 18-36-bit stuff is always referred to in the 18-bit world, the 36-bit world, and P?S/8 PAL as SIXBIT.  PAL8 and PAL10 do not support this.\line\line Here is why it is better:\line\line The octal code for A is 101.  The TEXT code is 01.  This continues with the rest of the letters, thus Z is 132, and TEXT is 32.   Same for many other codes.  Numeral codes are add 60 octal to the number, which is about equally slightly quirky in either code.\line\line But in SIXBIT, A is 41, not 01.  Thus, you have to do an extra mental step to see the correspondance.\line\line Also, PDP-8 code usually only has a common central output routine, while input could be in several places, thus the way the coding developed, it makes more sense, but they did what they did first.  The PDP-6/10/20 people just copied the 18-bit stuff, and they did what they did first on the PDP-1 in 1959/60 when no one really know much about longer-term standards.  Note: LINC does not use ASCII, rather it is a "throwback" code that is not quite compatible with the ancient CDC FIELDATA code I had some contact with at Western Union.  They just didn't bother with ASCII at all, but it's because it was not a released standard [just in final refereeing mode] when the LINC came out, thus they opted out on ASCII, etc.  But the DSC display [one half of a] character instruction could never handle a seven-bit character set, and it is pretty bad for a six-bit one!\par
