5)\tab The I directive changes meaning if LINC mode assembly is in \tab effect.  Since there are no indirect references in LINC \tab programming, the I directive is redefined to force bit[7] on.  In \tab the context of LINC addressing, the I directive means either \tab immediate mode (when used with instructions where the operand \tab immediately follows the instruction) or auto-indexed mode (when \tab used with references to locations 0001-0017).\page\cf5 Appendix B Notes (continued)\par
