Glossary of Terms\par
\par
1.\tab BIN format\par
\par
\tab The standard binary loading format in the DEC paper-tape\par
\tab operating system in known as \i BIN\i0  format which is optimized for\par
\tab loading data from paper-tape frames.  The format includes both\par
\tab leader and trailer frame codes to delineate sets of data; the\par
\tab highest tape channel is used as a visual aid to help position\par
\tab paper-tapes in the reader.  All leader frames are ignored. \tab Loading ends when the first trailer frame is encountered.  BIN\par
\tab format incorporates all necessary elements needed to load 12-bit\par
\tab words into memory including a 12-bit checksum at the end of the\par
\tab tape; memory field change frames are included as required.\line\line\tab BIN format is inherently compromised in terms of loading\par
\tab efficiency because of the need to support leader and checksum\par
\tab codes that are totally irrelevant in an operating system\par
\tab environment such as P?S/8.  Storage devices inherently have\par
\tab device parity checking for all data; leader and trailer frame\par
\tab codes serve no purpose when data is stored in blocks on a mass \tab storage device.\par
\par
\tab While certain systems use an adaptation of BIN format for binary\par
\tab files, P?S/8 uses the more versatile and efficient \i Slurp\i0  format \tab described elsewhere.  \i Slurp\i0  format is optimized for efficient\par
\tab loading; the P?S/8 binary utility program (also known as \i BIN\i0 ) \tab includes utilities to convert between paper-tape data and binary\par
 \tab file data (in both conversion directions).\par
\par
2.\tab Edit buffer\par
\par
\tab TFS text files are created in the edit buffer using commands of\par
\tab the keyboard monitor.  Files are tentative until the edit buffer\par
\tab contents are saved to a file in the TFS Directory.\par
\par
3.\tab Extended-length text files\par
\par
\tab Extended-length files partially resemble TFS text files except no\tab actual line number or line pointer data exists within the file.\par
\tab Additionally, there are no length restrictions on extended-length\par
\tab files (other than the logical or physical limits of the storage\par
\tab device).\par
\par
4.\tab Logical Console Overlay\par
\par
\tab The \ul\i Logical Console Overlay\ulnone\i0  is an optional component of P?S/8.\par
\tab When enabled, all conforming programs will direct all system\tab console (and lineprinter) input/output to designated memory\par
\tab locations instead of the usual physical device.  While the use of\par
\tab the Logical Console Overlay requires the availability of extended\par
\tab memory, certain advanced features are enabled as follows:\page\cf5 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0  \par
\par
\tab a) If the P?S/8 system device handler detects an error, the usual\tab    action is to halt the CPU (often displaying status bits in the\par
\tab    accumulator); pressing \highlight7 CONTINUE\highlight0  on the system front panel will\par
\tab    safely retry the latest read/write operation. Users need to be\par
\tab    familiar with the particular system device handler in use to\par
\tab    gain insight as to what caused the specific problem (if\par
\tab    possible) by examining the contents of the accumulator.\par
\par
\tab    Note: An available system option is to disable the system halt\par
\tab    which results in an infinite retry of all errors; depending on\par
\tab    hardware particulars, this setting is generally not\par
\tab    recommended.\par
\par
\tab    When the Logical Console Overlay is enabled, the action taken\par
\tab    on detection of an error by the system device handler becomes\par
\tab    a status report printed on the system console device (as\par
\tab    defined by the Logical Console Overlay which could include\par
\tab    alternate console hardware).  This includes details of the\par
\tab    latest read/write operation; the user can choose which action\par
\tab    to take at the point of error by entering one of the stated\par
\tab    options on the system console keyboard.  This allows the user\par
\tab    to correct the problem (if possible) and retry the latest\par
\tab    read/write operation, or to abort the latest operation (if\par
\tab    necessary).  Without requiring specific knowledge of the\par
\tab    vagaries of the current system device, any user can properly\par
\tab    manage the system device handler recovery operations.\par
\par
\tab b) Not every system device handler is capable of optimal\par
 \tab    performance; this is especially true of system device handlers\par
        designed to run on 4K systems (without requiring loading of a\par
        portion of the handler into extended memory).  Due to limited\par
        memory space, it is often necessary to compromise performance\par
\tab    to obtain basic functionality.\par
\par
        When the Logical Console Overlay is enabled, additional\par
        functionality (included in the system device handler for this\par
        purpose) is also enabled.  This generally effects additional\par
        performance that overcomes the basic design compromise.\par
\par
        Note: On hardware configurations where the system device\par
\tab    handler requires an extended-memory component, the Logical\par
\tab    Console Overlay is designed to function in the rest of the\par
\tab    same (highest) memory field.  As such, for conforming programs \tab    (such as P?S/8 PAL) attempting to locate extended memory\par
\tab    fields, the use of the Logical Console Overlay does not change\par
\tab    the number of complete memory fields available.  Under\par
\tab    certain circumstances, P?S/8 PAL may load additional symbols\par
 \tab    into the 3/4 memory field availale if the Logical Console\par
\tab    Overlay is not enabled.\page\cf5 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0  \par
\cf5\par
\tab\cf0 c) Certain configurations of the Logical Console Overlay may use\par
 \tab    alternate console hardware (such as an alternate console\par
\tab    terminal with device codes differing from 03/04).  In certain\par
\tab    configurations, an error panel screen may replace the normal\par
\tab    output of the stream of console output until the error is\par
\tab    resolved.\par
\par
5.\tab RIM format\par
\par
\tab While BIN format is the standard format for loading most binary\par
\tab paper-tape programs, \i RIM\i0  format is used to load the BIN loader.\par
\tab While RIM format is more inefficient, this is generally not an\par
\tab issue with relatively short programs.  Some users want a minimal\par
\tab overall solution to loading a particular program; as such, the \tab binary might be punched in RIM format, eliminating the need for\par
 \tab the bin loader as an intermediate program.\par
\par
\tab RIM format uses the same overall layout of the BIN loader; the\par
\tab differences are as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) Auto-update of the loading point is not supported; each loaded\par
\tab    word must include an origin setting before the data word.\par
\par
\tab b) No support for a checksum.\par
\par
\tab c) No support for FIELD settings.  The user must set the loading\par
\tab    field front the front panel Data Field switches.\par
\par
\tab d) Two equally short versions of the RIM loader exist.  One\par
\tab    supports the high-speed reader; the other supports the low-\par
\tab    speed reader of the model ASR 33 (or ASR 35) Teletype often\par
\tab    used as the system console device.  All versions of the BIN\par
\tab    loader are far longer; one of the RIM loader variants is used\par
\tab    to load the BIN loader.\par
\par
6.\tab\i Slurp\i0  binary file format and \i Slurp\i0  loader\par
\par
\tab\i Slurp \i0 is the name given to the P?S/8 binary file format by its\par
     inventor, Richard Lary.  Mr Lary and his colleague Lennie Elekman\par
\tab are the principal authors of the \ul\i R-L Monitor System\ulnone\i0 ; P?S/8 is\par
\tab (partially) based on this earlier system.  Both operating systems\par
\tab support \i Slurp\i0  format binary files as created by P?S/8 PAL and\par
\tab other utilities.\par
\par
\tab Note: The assembler used in the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  is the DEC\par
\tab paper-tape operating system assembler known as \ul\i PAL III\ulnone\i0 .  Certain \tab modifications were made to write binary output to the system\par
\tab device in \i Slurp\i0  binary format.  Although binary output is limited\par
\tab to designated system blocks, additional commands exist to \par
\tab transfer the binary output to user-designated \i Slurp\i0  format binary\par
\tab files in the TFS directory.  P?S/8 PAL supports \i Slurp\i0  format\par
\tab binary output files directly.\page\cf5 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0  \par
\par
\tab The fastest loading of binary data into randomly accessed memory\par
\tab locations is achieved using \i Slurp\i0  binary format.  Actual loading\par
\tab efficiency approaches 6/7 of block-oriented (core-image) binary\par
\tab format while allowing the loading of arbitrary data ranging from\par
\tab individual memory locations through large blocks of contiguous\par
\tab memory without requiring any form of block structure.  Memory can\par
\tab be freely loaded anywhere in the entire PDP-8 32K memory space\par
\tab (other than system reserved memory).\par
\par
\tab Note: All implementations of the \i Slurp\i0  loader prior to P?S/8 were\par
\tab limited to loading into field 0 only; P?S/8 uses an extension to\par
\tab the format that doesn't make its 4K usage any less efficient.  An \tab appropriate \i CDF XX\i0  instruction to the new memory loading field is\par
\tab required (only) when the loading field changes.\par
\par
\tab Note: Since the P?S/8 \i Slurp\i0  loader is a superset of the original\par
\tab 4K-only versions as used is several other PDP-8 systems, files\par
\tab transferred from the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  can be loaded without\par
\tab internal conversion.  Since P?S/8 PAL supports chaining to P?S/8\par
\tab\i BIN\i0 , all loading options of the \i Slurp\i0  loader are described\par
\tab elsewhere in this document.\par
\par
\tab The list of other operating systems that support the \i Slurp\i0  loader\par
\tab is as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) \ul The R-L Monitor System\ulnone  (also known as \i MS/8\i0  as submitted to the\par
\tab    \ul\i Digital Equipment Corporation User Society\ulnone\i0  (\i DECUS\i0 )).  This is\par
\tab    the earliest system to support a \i Slurp\i0  loader, albeit limited\par
\tab    to 4K loading.  This system did not provide a virtual \i Slurp\par
\i0\tab    loader option and is only implemented on TC01/TC08 DECtape for\par
\tab    a single drive unit.  Most notably, MS/8 is not a commercial\par
\tab    product, as it was entirely written by students of the\par
\tab    \ul\i Polytechnic Institute of Brooklyn\ulnone\i0  (now a division of \ul\i New York \par
\ulnone\i0\tab    \ul\i University\ulnone\i0 ).  Most of these students eventually became\par
\tab    employees of \ul\i Digital Equipment Corporation\ulnone\i0  (\i DEC\i0 ); however, all\par
\tab    work on the R-L Monitor System was performed by students in\par
\tab    the New York City area (including the author of this\par
\tab    document).\par
\par
\tab b) While DEC was totally unaware of this situation, \i DIBOL-8\i0  (the\par
\tab    precursor of \i COS-300/310\i0 ) was actually a hacked-up variant of\par
\tab    the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone ; binary output of the DIBOL compiler\tab\tab    (and the DIBOL run-time system) are loaded by the original\par
\tab    \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  \i Slurp \i0 loader.  The keyboard monitor\par
\tab    commands were modified to be consistent with the proposed COS\par
\tab    systems released somewhat later.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Early releases of P?S/8 are faithful to the original\par
\tab    keyboard monitor commands of the \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone ; however,\par
\tab    all recent releases are consistent with the DIBOL/COS systems.\par
\tab    Where relevant, documentation of the COS keyboard commands\par
\tab    also applies to P?S/8.\page\cf5 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0  \par
\par
\tab c) \ul\i POLY BASIC\ulnone\i0  is a stand-alone BASIC-only operation system for a\par
\tab    4K PDP-8 including any of several storage devices such as the\par
\tab    TC01/TC08 DECtape, PDP-12 LINCtape, DF32 and RF08.  It was\par
 \tab    developed on The \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  by the same students who\par
\tab    created the original \ul R-L Monitor System\ulnone  while still students\par
\tab    just before their employment at DEC in Maynard Mass.\par
\par
\tab    The internal run-time system and binary output of the BASIC\par
\tab    compiler are loaded into memory using a \i Slurp\i0  loader limited\par
\tab    to 4K memory.\par
\par
\tab    Note: For devices where a virtual loader is required in P?S/8\par
 \tab    (due to hardware limitations), POLY BASIC takes advantage of\par
\tab    an internal buffer known to be available while file loading is\par
 \tab    in progress.  (The buffer area is later used for dynamic\par
 \tab    program storage.)\par
\par
\tab    A minimal distribution cleanup and eventual submission to\par
\tab    DECUS was accomplished later when the (former) students were\par
\tab    DEC employees (as opposed to the bulk of the work done earlier\par
\tab    as students).\par
\par
\tab    DEC management unfairly exploited this situation and claimed\par
\tab    the project as DEC intellectual property without any\par
\tab    compensation to either the students or the school.  This\par
\tab    situation never sat well with certain individuals associated\par
\tab    with this incident; it was felt at the time that DEC was too\par
\tab    powerful to lodge any complaints against (and the inherent\par
\tab    intimidation caused by fear of being fired).\par
\par
\tab d) \ul\i EDUSYSTEM 30\ulnone\i0  is DEC's brazenly commercial release of \ul POLY\par
\ulnone  \tab    \ul BASIC\ulnone  with little to no modifications over the original\par
 \tab    student project.\par
\par
\tab e) \ul\i EDUSYSTEM 15/30\ulnone  \i0 is a specific variant of EDUSYSTEM 30 that\par
\tab    runs only on the TD8E DECtape.  Either 8K of memory or the\par
\tab    MR8E-C support ROM is required.\par
\par
\tab    Note: When the MR8E-C ROM is used, it is located in field 7 of\par
\tab    memory in locations 77400-77777.  As such, the host PDP-8/E\par
\tab    system must support extended memory for either configuration.\par
 \tab    It is believed that, out of expediency, the technique of using\par
\tab    an internal buffer during \i Slurp\i0  loading is deployed in this\par
\tab    system.  P?S/8 implements a TD8E-specific \i Slurp \i0 loader for the\par
\tab    same hardware configurations.\par
\par
\cf1 The new 7. goes here for three-for-two and then up the numbers on all past this!\line\par
\cf0 7.   Three-for-two\par
\par
      This is an informal reference to the method used in certain PDP-8 file systems pack three seven (or eight) bit characters into two adjacent 12-bit words within a larger structure (such as a storage device block).  This packing scheme is used in OS/8 binary output frames inside of binary files and text files in both OS/8 and P?S/8 SHELL environments.\line\line The first character occupies the low-order eight bits of the first twelve-bit word.  The second character occupies the low-order eight bits of the second twelve-bit word.  The third character is split into an upper-most four bits and a lower-most four bits.  The upper bits are placed into the four high-order bits of the first 12-bit word; the lower bits are placed into the four high-order bits of the second 12-bit word.\line\line While many less than satisfactory methods of dealing with three-for-two have been devised, clearly the most elegant way is to use co-routines such as how the author of this document implemented file support in the OS/8 program Kermit-12.  The code space occupied by both routines together is slightly less than half of that used by commonly used routines for the same purpose in many OS/8 programs, yet the co-routines are also faster and exhibit higher throughput.  This is one of the few counter-examples where speed and small size are not in tradeoff opposition. \cf1\line\cf0\par
\par
\cf1 8\cf0 .\tab Tiny File System (TFS) and the TFS directory\par
\par
\tab The \ul\i Tiny File System\ulnone\i0  (\i TFS\i0 ) is the primary file format used in the\par
 \tab basic P?S/8 system.  Each TFS file is of fixed size allowing the\par
\tab TFS directory structure to consist of allocatable slots that\par
\tab cannot overlap.\page\cf5 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0  \par
\par
 \tab If a TFS directory filename is modified or deleted, the\par
\tab underlying storage is not changed (unless an explicit write\par
\tab operation is performed).  This allows files to be easily renamed\par
\tab as needed.\par
\par
\tab There are unique advantages to the TFS structure which, for\par
\tab certain users, requires some measure of orientation and \par
\tab experience.  Once mastered, rapid editing of large projects can\par
\tab proceed with little additional effort.\line\line\tab Files are loaded into the keyboard monitor edit buffer to modify\par
\tab the contents.  The process is not finalized until appropriate\par
\tab commands are issued to write the edit buffer contents to a slot \tab in the TFS directory.  This generally means that the same file \tab slot is reused; as such, there will be no need to access the TFS\par
\tab directory.\par
\par
\tab Note: For those requiring the notion of a file backup, the\par
\tab updated file can be written to a different TFS directory slot.\par
\tab Appropriate filename changes can be made later to maintain\par
\tab ongoing file names.  Advanced users are known to pre-allocate \tab additional directory slots for this purpose.\par
\par
\tab The TFS directory structure is used only for user-created files,\par
\tab including the \i Slurp\i0  binary files produced by P?S/8 PAL assembly\par
\tab of TFS text files (and/or extended-length text files).  System\par
\tab programs are stored in an independent file structure elsewhere on \par
\tab the system device and/or other logical device units.\par
\par
\tab It is expected that PAL assembly projects will require multiple\par
\tab files.  The contents of multiple files are concatenated together\par
\tab and passed to P?S/8 PAL as required to carry out the current\par
\tab assembly project.\par
\par
\tab Note: While certain P?S/8 system programs address the associated\par
\tab line numbers within TFS text files, P?S/8 PAL ignores line number\par
\tab data.\par
\par
